Found by fuzz-differential once the generator emitted server steps (bd-86np), which shifted the random stream: 2 of 6000 (seed 17 gen-17-81, seed 29 gen-29-127), both minimized by twin_run minimize; neither depends on servers. Trace class, snapshot and observations match; only the lab dispatch order differs, so the divergence is in which tasks a timer fire (or a cancel) wakes and in what order.

(1) gen-17-81-min: t.1 races one member (sleep 250) with deadline_ns 250; the script cancels t.1 (Timeout) at 150. At 250 Rust dispatches t.1/g1.1, t.1/g1.timeout, t.1; C dispatches t.1 first (an extra owner wake, likely its deadline sleep firing although Rust's owner does not get woken by it after the cancel), then g1.1, timeout, t.1.
(2) gen-29-127-min: t.2 races three members (yield+sleep 150, sleep 200, sleep 200) with deadline_ns 100; the script cancels r.main (Deadline) at 50. At 200 Rust dispatches g3.2, timeout, g3.3, timeout; C dispatches g3.3, g3.2, timeout, t.2 (equal-deadline member timers in another order, and the wrapper's wakes merged).

Start from exec_race_deadline (src/conformance/interpreter.c) and Scope::timeout (asupersync cx/scope.rs:2130-2196): what the owner's deadline sleep does after the owner is cancelled (dropped? still registered with a stale waker?), and how equal-deadline timers registered in one step are ordered. Minimized scenarios (asx.scenario.v2, captured fine by twin_run; C FAIL dispatches):
